WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.433
A schema describes the shape of your data: the kinds of records, their fields, and the

00:00:05.433 --> 00:00:10.741
relationships between them. Here, a shipment has a status and a customer identifier,

00:00:10.741 --> 00:00:16.049
and it has a list of related events. A field map describes that shape to the engine.

00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:21.419
Before asking an agent to query anything, we can give it a smaller, permitted surface

00:00:21.419 --> 00:00:24.200
of this schema. We call that surface a lens.

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:34.446
A lens describes what can be seen and queried: which fields, which relationships, and

00:00:34.446 --> 00:00:40.193
which records are available. Here, the platform omits internal cost. The other fields

00:00:40.193 --> 00:00:45.805
remain usable. This is more than hiding a column in the interface. A predicate that

00:00:45.805 --> 00:00:51.551
tries to use the excluded field also fails validation. The surface shown to the agent

00:00:51.551 --> 00:00:55.000
and the boundary enforced by the engine must agree.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:05.027
Limiting fields answers what information is available. Limiting records answers whose

00:01:05.027 --> 00:01:10.100
information is available. The platform binds an organization identity into the

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:15.302
inherited rule. The organization then binds the customer identity. Binding means

00:01:15.302 --> 00:01:20.830
supplying a trusted value for a named input. The agent does not choose that identity.

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:26.163
Our customer can ask flexible questions, but those questions are combined with the

00:01:26.163 --> 00:01:29.350
ownership conditions supplied by the application.

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:38.226
Imagine three sets, each inside the previous one: the platform’s data, an

00:01:38.226 --> 00:01:42.842
organization’s data, and a customer’s data within that organization. When the

00:01:42.842 --> 00:01:47.577
organization delegates its lens, it can preserve the existing view or narrow it

00:01:47.577 --> 00:01:52.433
further. It cannot widen it. This property is called monotonic narrowing. Along a

00:01:52.433 --> 00:01:57.229
delegation chain, restrictions accumulate; they do not disappear. A child cannot

00:01:57.229 --> 00:02:02.024
restore a field its parent removed or cancel its inherited ownership conditions.

00:02:06.524 --> 00:02:11.642
Before writing a query, an agent should be able to ask: which lenses are available to

00:02:11.642 --> 00:02:16.518
me? Which fields and relationships can I use? What values does status accept? The

00:02:16.518 --> 00:02:21.454
catalog and field maps supply those answers. They describe structure and available

00:02:21.454 --> 00:02:26.632
options; your application should also supply business meanings where needed. These are

00:02:26.632 --> 00:02:31.688
prepared discovery examples grounded in the actual exposed surface. The agent should

00:02:31.688 --> 00:02:35.000
not guess a field or infer access from a friendly name.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:44.464
Now that the agent knows its surface, it can form a more detailed question. This

00:02:44.464 --> 00:02:49.678
predicate asks for at least two related events, each of which is a customs hold with

00:02:49.678 --> 00:02:55.015
twelve or more delay hours. All means both tests must match the same event. The engine

00:02:55.015 --> 00:03:00.042
validates these references against the lens, combines the question with inherited

00:03:00.042 --> 00:03:05.379
restrictions, and builds the database plan. The grouping step counts qualifying events

00:03:05.379 --> 00:03:07.800
before matching shipments are returned.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:17.803
Suppose that predicate was valid yesterday. Today, the platform removes delay hours

00:03:17.803 --> 00:03:23.306
from the fields its lens exposes. The organization and customer inherit the change,

00:03:23.306 --> 00:03:28.876
but the old predicate still refers to delay hours. The rule is now invalid under the

00:03:28.876 --> 00:03:34.246
lens it was based on. That is rule staleness. The validator identifies the broken

00:03:34.246 --> 00:03:39.948
reference. Re-running the same predicate cannot repair it; the rule must be revised to

00:03:39.948 --> 00:03:41.275
fit the new surface.

00:03:45.775 --> 00:03:50.944
Try asking for internal cost, or adding an always-true branch to a question about

00:03:50.944 --> 00:03:56.433
another customer. The hidden field is rejected, and the permissive branch still cannot

00:03:56.433 --> 00:04:01.858
remove the trusted ownership conditions. That is why delegation is useful for agents:

00:04:01.858 --> 00:04:07.219
they can explore the surface they receive, while the engine preserves its limits. We

00:04:07.219 --> 00:04:12.644
have explained what a lens contains. Next we need to decide who is allowed to receive

00:04:12.644 --> 00:04:12.900
one.
